purposes:
  - response: >-
      To create and publicly share a flagship, ethically sourced, AI-ready multimodal dataset on
      type 2 diabetes mellitus that enables future generations of artificial intelligence and
      machine learning research, including research into salutogenesis (the pathway from disease
      back to health).
  - response: >-
      To provide data specifically designed to permit downstream pseudotime manifold analysis, an
      approach used to predict disease trajectories by learning from complex multimodal data
      collected from participants with differing disease severity, from no diabetes to
      insulin-dependent T2DM, and thereby to reconstruct a temporal atlas of T2DM development and
      reversal towards health.
  - response: >-
      To establish and disseminate a blueprint of standards, best practices and guidelines for the
      collection, preparation and sharing of AI-ready biomedical datasets following the FAIR
      (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, Reusable) principles, as a model for future data
      generation projects.
  - response: >-
      To develop a foundational dataset in type 2 diabetes that is agnostic to existing
      classification criteria or biases and hypothesis agnostic, spanning over ten health and
      behavioural domains likely to be impacted by T2DM.

addressing_gaps:
  - response: >-
      The ability to understand and affect the course of complex, multi-organ diseases such as
      T2DM has been limited by a lack of well-designed, high-quality, large and inclusive
      multimodal datasets; a major barrier to AI in this area has been the lack of diverse datasets
      that are AI ready for training models.
  - response: >-
      A meta-epidemiological review of type 2 diabetes studies published from 2000 to 2020 found
      that samples lacked racial and ethnic diversity. The cohort is deliberately balanced across
      Asian, Black, Hispanic and White participants rather than mirroring the US demographic
      distribution, addressing under-representation of groups that bear a higher burden of disease.
  - response: >-
      Existing data sources such as insurance claims or electronic health records cannot support
      the intended downstream AI/ML analyses, so a purpose-built multi-domain acquisition protocol
      spanning survey, physical, clinical, imaging and wearable device data was designed.
  - response: >-
      Preparing and sharing AI-ready datasets is time consuming and difficult for data-collecting
      researchers; the project addresses this by developing the FAIRhub curation and sharing
      platform and new standards such as the Clinical Dataset Structure (CDS).

tasks:
  - response: >-
      Development and training of artificial intelligence and machine learning models on multimodal
      type 2 diabetes data.
  - response: >-
      Pseudotime manifold analysis to predict disease trajectories and reconstruct a temporal atlas
      of T2DM pathogenesis and salutogenesis.
  - response: >-
      Research into salutogenesis, that is, the pathway from type 2 diabetes back to health.

intended_uses:
  - usage_notes: >-
      Development of AI/ML models, including multimodal models, that improve understanding of type
      2 diabetes mellitus, its complications and its reversal toward health.
    examples:
      - Multimodal AI model development across survey, clinical, imaging, waveform and wearable data
      - Prediction of disease trajectories via pseudotime manifold analysis
  - usage_notes: >-
      Type 2 diabetes related research generally. Access to the public dataset requires the user to
      agree to use the data only for type 2 diabetes related research; a controlled-access version
      obtained under a data use agreement permits more generic use.
  - usage_notes: >-
      Use as a worked example and blueprint for other data generation projects seeking to produce
      FAIR, AI-ready clinical research datasets, including reuse of the Clinical Dataset Structure
      organisation and the standards mappings applied to each data domain.

