anomalies:
  - anomaly_details: >-
      Not all modalities are available for all participants. Causes include participants electing not
      to take part in some study elements, devices with no stored results or returned too late to
      retrieve results (for example battery failure or lost data), and occasional data collisions
      during processing that resulted in lost data.
  - anomaly_details: >-
      In survey data, skipped questions and incomplete responses are expected.
  - anomaly_details: >-
      In wearable device data, improper use and technical failures such as battery failure or system
      malfunction are expected.
  - anomaly_details: >-
      In imaging data, participant non-cooperation, noise that may obscure the images, system
      malfunction and data transfer failures are expected.
  - anomaly_details: >-
      Image quality from the Optomed Aurora handheld camera varies from participant to participant and
      within the same participant, because images are acquired undilated with a handheld device.
  - anomaly_details: >-
      Fluorescence lifetime imaging ophthalmoscopy is performed as the last modality of the visit
      because it takes longer than a standard OCT and exposes participants to uncomfortable levels of
      light; participant compliance may therefore be lower for this modality.
  - anomaly_details: >-
      Imaging performed on operator-dependent devices such as the Heidelberg Spectralis may have
      quality issues that led to the modality being skipped and data being missing.
  - anomaly_details: >-
      Because enrolment is ongoing, the pilot data release and periodic updates to data releases may
      not have achieved the intended balanced distribution across groups.

missing_data_documentation:
  - missing_data_patterns: >-
      Missing data occur at the level of whole modalities for individual participants, and at the item
      level within surveys.
    missing_data_causes: >-
      Participants electing not to participate in some study elements; data collection devices with no
      stored results or returned too late (battery died, data lost); data collisions resulting in lost
      data; skipped or incomplete survey responses; and imaging sessions skipped because of quality
      problems or participant non-cooperation.
    handling_strategy: >-
      During cleaning, missing data that could be directly filled from other portions of an
      individual's record were completed. If there was no logical or appropriate way to correct the
      data, the site principal investigator reviewed the values and decided whether they should be
      removed. No imputation was performed.

cleaning_strategies:
  - cleaning_details: >-
      Quality control at the time of data entry and acquisition: clinical data outside expected
      minimum/maximum ranges were flagged in REDCap and appeared in reports viewed by clinical research
      coordinators and data managers. Using these reports, data managers and coordinators examined
      participant records and determined whether an error was likely. Data were checked and edited for
      credibility based on range checks; incorrect flow through prescribed skip patterns; missing data
      that could be directly filled from other portions of an individual's record; and the omission
      and/or duplication of records. Editing was done only under the guidance and approval of the site
      principal investigator.
  - cleaning_details: >-
      Central quality control: the data manager at each data site was responsible for the quality
      control of all data before uploading to FAIRhub, and the dataset was reviewed internally by
      AI-READI team members for quality checks and to ensure that no personally identifiable
      information was accidentally included. The dataset has not undergone any formal external audit.

preprocessing_strategies:
  - preprocessing_details: >-
      Mapping to standardized terminologies and data models. Clinical data collected through REDCap
      were mapped to the Observational Medical Outcomes Partnership (OMOP) Common Data Model, with each
      released CSV file corresponding one-to-one to an OMOP CDM table, and a dqd_omop.json file
      supporting the OMOP CDM Data Quality Dashboard checks.
  - preprocessing_details: >-
      Conversion of proprietary imaging formats to DICOM. Images exported from the Topcon Maestro2 and
      Triton in .fda format and from the Heidelberg FLIO in .sdt format were converted to DICOM for the
      dataset; images from the Optomed Aurora, iCare EIDON, Heidelberg Spectralis and Zeiss Cirrus were
      exported in DICOM.
  - preprocessing_details: >-
      Conversion of device time-series data to interoperability standards: Garmin Vivosmart 5 data
      exported in .FIT format were converted to the Open mHealth standard, Dexcom G6 continuous glucose
      data are provided following Open mHealth, and 12-lead ECG data exported as .xml were converted to
      the WaveForm DataBase (WFDB) standard. Environmental sensor data follow the NASA ASCII file format
      guidelines for Earth science data.
  - preprocessing_details: >-
      Organisation of the released dataset according to the Clinical Dataset Structure (CDS) v0.1.1
      specification: one directory per datatype at the root, then one directory per modality, then one
      per device, then one per participant, with manifest.tsv files and root-level metadata files
      (dataset_description.json, dataset_structure_description.json, participants.tsv, README.md,
      CHANGELOG.md, healthsheet.md, LICENSE.txt, study_description.json).
  - preprocessing_details: >-
      Removal of sensitive content for public release: all protected health information as defined by
      the HIPAA Privacy Rule was removed using the Safe Harbor method, and information related to the
      sex and race/ethnicity of participants and to medications used was also removed, in part to
      prevent stigmatization of findings.
  - preprocessing_details: >-
      Version-specific change: the Snellen visual acuity variables present in the previous version were
      dropped, so that only logMAR visual acuity measurements are available in version 3.0.0.
  - preprocessing_details: >-
      Processing maturity has changed across versions: version 1.0.0 (pilot, 204 participants) used
      custom and ad hoc processing, while the year-2 and year-3 data included in versions 2.0.0 and
      3.0.0 used a combination of automated and custom processing.

raw_sources:
  - raw_data_details: >-
      Device-native and platform-native raw exports, including ECG .xml, Topcon .fda, Heidelberg FLIO
      .sdt, Garmin .FIT, MoCA Duo .csv, laboratory .csv files and REDCap exports. The raw data are saved
      and expected to be preserved by the AI-READI project at least for the duration of the project but
      are not currently shared outside the project team, because they have not been mapped to
      standardized terminologies and because they may accidentally include personal health information
      or personally identifiable information, for example in free-text fields. Raw data may be included
      in future releases of the controlled-access dataset.

raw_data_sources:
  - source_description: >-
      Prospective in-person study visit data collected from participants at three sites (University of
      Alabama at Birmingham, University of California San Diego, University of Washington), covering
      questionnaires, physical measurements, vitals, ECG, monofilament testing, cognitive screening,
      vision testing and retinal imaging.
    raw_data_format: REDCap exports (.csv), ECG .xml, DICOM, .fda and .sdt proprietary imaging exports
  - source_description: >-
      Electronic health records at the three health systems, screened for ICD-10 diagnosis codes R73.09
      (pre-diabetes) and E11.x (type 2 diabetes) plus demographic data, used to identify recruitment
      pools of patients aged 40 years and over with an encounter between 2020 and 2025.
    access_details: >-
      Used for recruitment and screening only; the identified contact pools were used to generate mailed
      and emailed invitations directing individuals into the REDCap recruitment interface.
  - source_description: >-
      At-home monitoring devices worn or deployed for 10 days after the visit: Dexcom G6 continuous
      glucose monitor, Garmin Vivosmart 5 physical activity monitor and the custom LeeLab Anura home
      environmental sensor.
    raw_data_format: .csv (Dexcom and environmental sensor) and .FIT (Garmin)
  - source_description: >-
      Clinical laboratory testing of non-fasting blood (approximately 53 mL) and urine collected during
      the visit: complete blood count by CLIA-certified laboratories local to each site on fresh whole
      blood, and all other assays by the University of Washington Nutrition and Obesity Research Center
      on stored frozen samples.
    raw_data_format: .csv

variables:
  - variable_name: participant_id
    data_type: identifier
    is_identifier: true
    description: >-
      Study identifier used to name each participant directory and to link records across datatypes;
      recorded in participants.tsv. No direct personal identifiers are included in the dataset.
  - variable_name: diabetes_study_group
    data_type: categorical
    categories:
      - No diabetes (no type 1 or type 2 diabetes)
      - Pre-diabetes or type 2 diabetes controlled by lifestyle adjustments
      - Type 2 diabetes controlled by oral or non-insulin injectable medications
      - Type 2 diabetes controlled by insulin
    description: >-
      Diabetes severity group used for cohort balancing and for the recommended data splits.
  - variable_name: recommended_split
    data_type: categorical
    categories:
      - train
      - val
      - test
    description: >-
      Recommended train/validation/test assignment provided in participants.tsv, in 70/15/15
      proportions, balanced as far as possible for sex, race/ethnicity and diabetes status.
  - variable_name: age
    data_type: integer
    unit: years
    minimum_value: 40
    maximum_value: 85
    description: >-
      Participant age. Study eligibility requires age 40 years or older, with adults older than 85
      excluded. Mean age across the cohort is 60.8 years (standard deviation 11.3).
  - variable_name: hba1c
    data_type: float
    unit: '%'
    measurement_technique: >-
      Whole blood assay performed by the University of Washington Nutrition and Obesity Research Center
      on stored frozen samples.
    quality_notes: Laboratory reference range 4.0-6.0 per cent. Included as a marker for diabetes.
  - variable_name: glucose
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/dL
    measurement_technique: Serum assay performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Laboratory reference range 62-125 mg/dL. Included as a marker for diabetes.
  - variable_name: insulin
    data_type: float
    unit: ng/mL
    measurement_technique: EDTA plasma assay performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Laboratory reference range 0.0-24.9 ng/mL. Included as a marker for diabetes.
  - variable_name: c_peptide
    data_type: float
    unit: ng/mL
    measurement_technique: EDTA plasma assay performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Laboratory reference range 1.1-4.4 ng/mL. Indicator of insulin production.
  - variable_name: nt_probnp
    data_type: float
    unit: pg/mL
    measurement_technique: EDTA plasma assay performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: >-
      N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide; reference range varies by age. Severity and outcome
      predictor of heart failure.
  - variable_name: troponin_t
    data_type: float
    unit: ng/L
    measurement_technique: EDTA plasma assay performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Reference range female <11, male <16. Marker of myocardial injury.
  - variable_name: c_reactive_protein_high_sensitivity
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/L
    measurement_technique: Serum assay performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Reference range 0.0-10.0 mg/L. Inflammation marker and risk factor for T2DM.
  - variable_name: total_cholesterol
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/dL
    measurement_technique: Serum lipid panel performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Reference range <200 mg/dL. Cardiovascular disease risk factor.
  - variable_name: triglycerides
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/dL
    measurement_technique: Serum lipid panel performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Reference range <150 mg/dL. Cardiovascular disease risk factor.
  - variable_name: hdl_cholesterol
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/dL
    measurement_technique: Serum lipid panel performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Reference range >39 mg/dL. Cardiovascular disease risk factor.
  - variable_name: ldl_cholesterol
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/dL
    derivation: Calculated from the measured lipid panel values.
    quality_notes: Reference range <130 mg/dL. Cardiovascular disease risk factor.
  - variable_name: creatinine
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/dL
    measurement_technique: Serum assay performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Reference range female 0.38-1.02, male 0.51-1.18 mg/dL. Marker for kidney function.
  - variable_name: blood_urea_nitrogen
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/dL
    measurement_technique: Serum assay performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Reference range 8.0-21.0 mg/dL. Marker for kidney and liver function.
  - variable_name: urine_albumin
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/dL
    measurement_technique: Urine assay performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Urinary biomarker for early diabetic nephropathy; no reference range provided.
  - variable_name: urine_creatinine
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/dL
    measurement_technique: Urine assay performed by the UW Nutrition and Obesity Research Center.
    quality_notes: Urinary biomarker for early diabetic nephropathy; no reference range provided.
  - variable_name: white_blood_cell_count
    data_type: float
    unit: x10E3/uL
    measurement_technique: >-
      Complete blood count performed on fresh whole blood by a CLIA-certified laboratory local to each
      data collection site.
    quality_notes: Reference range 3.4-10.8 x10E3/uL.
  - variable_name: continuous_blood_glucose
    data_type: float
    unit: mg/dL
    measurement_technique: >-
      Dexcom G6 continuous glucose monitor, sampled every 5 minutes over a 10-day at-home monitoring
      period; participants were masked to the readings during monitoring.
  - variable_name: step_count
    data_type: integer
    measurement_technique: Garmin Vivosmart 5 physical activity monitor worn for 10 days.
  - variable_name: heart_rate
    data_type: integer
    unit: beats per minute
    measurement_technique: >-
      Measured at the visit with an automatic oscillometric device (twice, separated by 2 minutes) and
      continuously at home with the Garmin Vivosmart 5.
  - variable_name: oxygen_saturation
    data_type: float
    unit: '%'
    measurement_technique: Garmin Vivosmart 5 physical activity monitor worn for 10 days.
  - variable_name: sleep_duration
    data_type: float
    measurement_technique: >-
      Garmin Vivosmart 5 physical activity monitor; sleep duration and phases recorded over the 10-day
      at-home monitoring period, supporting circadian and diurnal rhythm analysis.
  - variable_name: systolic_blood_pressure
    data_type: integer
    unit: mm Hg
    measurement_technique: >-
      Automatic oscillometric, medically approved device (OMRON HEM 907XL, Medline MDS4001 or Welch
      Allyn 6000 series); measured twice separated by 2 minutes.
  - variable_name: diastolic_blood_pressure
    data_type: integer
    unit: mm Hg
    measurement_technique: >-
      Automatic oscillometric, medically approved device; measured twice separated by 2 minutes.
  - variable_name: body_mass_index
    data_type: float
    unit: kg/m2
    derivation: Calculated from measured height and weight.
  - variable_name: waist_hip_ratio
    data_type: float
    derivation: Calculated from measured waist and hip circumferences.
  - variable_name: moca_total_score
    data_type: integer
    minimum_value: 0
    maximum_value: 30
    measurement_technique: >-
      Montreal Cognitive Assessment administered electronically on an iPad using the MoCA Duo
      application; total score, section subscores, Memory Index Score and task completion times are
      recorded.
    quality_notes: >-
      Higher numbers represent better performance; total possible score is 30. Scores may be affected by
      education level, socioeconomic factors and mental health conditions.
  - variable_name: best_corrected_visual_acuity_logmar
    data_type: float
    unit: logMAR
    measurement_technique: >-
      M&S Technologies Electronic Visual Acuity tester at 4 metres, right and left eyes tested
      separately, under photopic conditions and under mesopic conditions using a 2.0 neutral density
      filter.
    quality_notes: >-
      Expressed as the logarithm of the minimum angle of resolution; lower numbers indicate better
      resolution. Snellen visual acuity variables present in earlier versions were dropped in v3.0.0.
  - variable_name: letter_contrast_sensitivity_logcs
    data_type: float
    unit: log contrast sensitivity
    measurement_technique: >-
      Mars letter contrast sensitivity chart read monocularly at approximately 50 cm under photopic
      conditions and under mesopic conditions using a neutral density occluder; testing terminated after
      two consecutive errors or at the end of the chart.
    quality_notes: Higher numbers indicate better sensitivity.
  - variable_name: monofilament_test_response
    data_type: categorical
    categories:
      - 'yes'
      - 'no'
    measurement_technique: >-
      Standard 10 g monofilament applied with the participant's eyes closed to three locations on the
      plantar surface of each foot, ten times per location; insensate areas retested for confirmation.
    description: Assessment of touch perception for peripheral neuropathy.
  - variable_name: ambient_temperature
    data_type: float
    measurement_technique: LeeLab Anura home environmental sensor, deployed in the home for 10 days.
  - variable_name: relative_humidity
    data_type: float
    unit: '%'
    measurement_technique: LeeLab Anura home environmental sensor, deployed in the home for 10 days.
  - variable_name: particulate_matter
    data_type: float
    measurement_technique: >-
      LeeLab Anura home environmental sensor; mass concentrations reported for PM1.0, PM2.5, PM4 and
      PM10.
  - variable_name: nitrogen_oxides
    data_type: float
    measurement_technique: >-
      LeeLab Anura home environmental sensor; nitrogen oxides (NO and NO2), reported using the Sensirion
      NOx index.
  - variable_name: volatile_organic_compounds
    data_type: float
    measurement_technique: LeeLab Anura home environmental sensor.
  - variable_name: multispectral_light_intensity
    data_type: array
    measurement_technique: >-
      LeeLab Anura home environmental sensor; eleven multi-spectral light intensity measurements.

known_biases:
  - bias_type: selection_bias
    bias_description: >-
      Participants are volunteers, so there is selection bias known as volunteer bias, which may limit
      the generalisability of results to those who are not represented in the study population.
    mitigation_strategy: >-
      Recruitment in waves with monitoring of the observed composition of participants, and adjustment
      by under- and oversampling groups as needed.
  - bias_type: sampling_bias
    bias_description: >-
      Recruitment was predominantly urban and hospital-based, drawing from patients with an encounter in
      one of three academic health systems, and may not fully capture diverse cultural and socioeconomic
      backgrounds.
    mitigation_strategy: >-
      Three recording sites were strategically selected for geographic diversity across the United
      States and to ensure diverse representation across racial and ethnic groups; individuals from all
      demographic backgrounds were recruited at all three sites, and uniform data collection protocols
      were used at all sites.
  - bias_type: representation_bias
    bias_description: >-
      The cohort does not include groups such as Pacific Islanders and Native Americans, and the
      requirement that participants speak and read English excludes non-English speakers and may have
      created challenges in recruiting Hispanic and/or Asian persons. Pregnancy, gestational diabetes,
      type 1 diabetes and age over 85 are exclusionary.
    mitigation_strategy: >-
      Through engagement with the Native Biodata Consortium the project pursues a co-learning opportunity
      for understanding challenges specific to American Indian and Alaska Native communities; new
      recruitment strategies were planned if race/ethnicity balancing emerged as a challenge.
    affected_subsets: >-
      Non-English speakers; Pacific Islander and Native American populations; pregnant people; people
      with type 1 or gestational diabetes; adults over 85.
  - bias_type: representation_bias
    bias_description: >-
      Although the study aims for balance across race/ethnicity, biological sex and diabetes severity,
      enrolment is ongoing and the released versions, beginning with the pilot, may not have achieved
      balanced distribution across these groups.
    mitigation_strategy: >-
      Recommended training/validation/testing splits are provided in which the validation and test sets
      are balanced as far as possible for sex, race/ethnicity and diabetes status, specifically to reduce
      disparities in models developed with the dataset.
  - bias_type: annotation_bias
    bias_description: >-
      Historical under-recruitment of Black men, Hispanic and Asian persons into health research, driven
      in part by medical mistrust stemming from historical events such as the Tuskegee syphilis study, is
      documented as a challenge that could skew the achieved sample relative to the intended balanced
      design.
    mitigation_strategy: >-
      Tailoring of printed materials and use of a personalised, participatory approach to recruitment;
      a Community Advisory Board of 11 persons from the three sites, including diversity in race and
      ethnicity as represented in the sample, contributes to protocol development.

known_limitations:
  - limitation_type: temporal_limitation
    limitation_description: >-
      The study is cross-sectional and this version contains only one study visit per participant, so
      the data do not directly support longitudinal analysis of individual trajectories.
    recommended_mitigation: >-
      Downstream pseudotime manifold analysis is the intended approach for inferring trajectories from
      cross-sectional data across the severity spectrum; a longitudinal arm inviting approximately 10 per
      cent of the cohort back in year 4 is planned.
    scope_impact: Applies to the entire dataset.
  - limitation_type: coverage_limitation
    limitation_description: >-
      The public dataset excludes sex, race, ethnicity, medications, 5-digit zip code, genetic sequencing
      data, past health records, and traffic and accident reports; these are available only under
      controlled access.
    recommended_mitigation: >-
      Users needing these variables must enter into a data use agreement to obtain the controlled-access
      version.
    scope_impact: Applies to the entire public dataset.
  - limitation_type: representativeness_limitation
    limitation_description: >-
      The cohort is deliberately balanced rather than representative of the US population, and does not
      include all racial and ethnic groups; it is drawn from three urban academic health systems.
    scope_impact: Affects the external validity of models trained on the dataset.
  - limitation_type: scope_limitation
    limitation_description: >-
      Access to the public dataset is conditional on agreeing to use the data only for type 2 diabetes
      related research, which constrains the range of secondary analyses permitted with the public
      release.
    recommended_mitigation: >-
      A private/controlled version obtained under a data use agreement allows for more generic use.
  - limitation_type: coverage_limitation
    limitation_description: >-
      Snellen visual acuity variables available in the previous version were dropped, so only logMAR
      visual acuity measurements are available in version 3.0.0.
    scope_impact: Affects comparisons between version 3.0.0 and earlier versions.
  - limitation_type: methodological_limitation
    limitation_description: >-
      A limitation of the associated biorepository is that there are a finite number of samples to share
      with scientists interested in using them in research; procedures for reviewing and prioritising
      written requests were still to be developed at the time of the protocol publication. The
      biospecimens are not part of this dataset.
  - limitation_type: methodological_limitation
    limitation_description: >-
      No explicit labels or targets are provided and no human or machine annotation was performed, so the
      dataset provides no gold-standard labels and no inter-annotator agreement information; there are
      also no formal guidelines for future researchers creating new labels or defining new tasks.

content_warnings:
  - content_warnings_present: false
    description: >-
      The healthsheet states that the dataset does not contain data that, if viewed directly, might be
      offensive, insulting, threatening, or otherwise pose a safety risk such as psychological safety and
      anxiety.

sensitive_elements:
  - sensitive_elements_present: false
    sensitivity_details: >-
      The public dataset does not contain data considered sensitive: it contains no protected health
      information, and information related to sex, race/ethnicity and medications used has been removed.
  - sensitive_elements_present: true
    sensitivity_details: >-
      The controlled-access dataset contains sensitive elements: 5-digit zip code, sex, race, ethnicity,
      genetic sequencing data, past health records, medications, and traffic and accident reports. These
      are available only to users whose institutions have completed legal and privacy use agreements with
      the AI-READI research programme.

confidential_elements:
  - confidential_elements_present: false
    confidentiality_details: >-
      The healthsheet states that the dataset does not contain data that might be considered confidential
      and that no personally identifiable information is included.

is_deidentified:
  identifiable_elements_present: false
  deidentification_details: >-
    The DataCite metadata records the de-identification type as "NoDeIdentification" with the explanation
    that no identifiers were collected, so no active de-identification was necessary, but the team checked
    that no identifiable data per US HIPAA were present in the data; direct identifiers and HIPAA
    identifiers are flagged as addressed while date shifting, non-arrangement and k-anonymity techniques
    were not applied. The public set is stripped of Protected Health Information as defined by the HIPAA
    Privacy Rule using the Safe Harbor method, and information related to sex and race/ethnicity is also
    removed to prevent stigmatization of findings. To prevent re-identification attempts, the project
    implements a robust data dissemination system, a strict licence agreement and data watermarking for
    both public and controlled sets. A theoretical risk of future re-identification is acknowledged.

human_subject_research:
  involves_human_subjects: true
  ethics_review_board: >-
    Institutional Review Board of the University of Washington, with reliance agreements from the IRBs of
    the University of Alabama at Birmingham and the University of California, San Diego.
  irb_approval: >-
    University of Washington IRB approval number STUDY00016228; initial approval received 20 December
    2022, with annual renewal applications required within 90 days of expiration. The FAIRhub study
    metadata records the human subject review status as "Submitted, approved".
  regulatory_compliance: >-
    Written informed consent is provided by all participants. Public release complies with the HIPAA
    Privacy Rule Safe Harbor method. Licensees must comply with the data security and privacy standards
    established under the NIH Genomic Data Sharing (GDS) Policy. The study is registered on
    ClinicalTrials.gov as NCT06002048. The FAIRhub study metadata records that the study is not an
    FDA-regulated drug or device study and has no Data Monitoring Committee.
  special_populations: >-
    Only adults able to provide their own consent are enrolled (aged 40 years and older, with adults over
    85 excluded). Pregnant people, people with gestational diabetes and people with type 1 diabetes are
    excluded. No minors are enrolled.

ethical_reviews:
  - reviewing_organization: Institutional Review Board of the University of Washington
    review_details: >-
      AI-READI was approved by the University of Washington IRB under approval number STUDY00016228,
      including reliance agreements with the IRBs of the University of Alabama at Birmingham and the
      University of California, San Diego. The initial approval letter is dated 20 December 2022 and is
      published with the dataset documentation; an annual renewal application reporting the status and
      progress of the study is required.
  - reviewing_organization: AI-READI ethics and bioethics contributors
    review_details: >-
      Ethical, legal and social implications are considered at every stage of the project cycle, with
      guidance from bioethicists. The project engages with the Native Biodata Consortium as a co-learning
      opportunity for understanding challenges specific to American Indian and Alaska Native communities
      and for evaluating tools such as data usage agreements, contract negotiations, transparent access
      agreements and assured return of benefit and sustainable input, control and monitoring of tribal
      data.
  - reviewing_organization: AI-READI Community Advisory Board
    review_details: >-
      A Community Advisory Board of 11 persons drawn from the three sites, including diversity in race and
      ethnicity as represented in the AI-READI sample, contributes to the development of the protocol and
      evaluates consent forms and research protocols.

informed_consent:
  - consent_obtained: true
    consent_scope: >-
      Informed consent to participate was required before participation in any part of the protocol,
      including questionnaires. The consent covers collection of the multimodal study data, biospecimen
      collection and biobanking for future research, and placement of data in an open-source repository.
    consent_documentation: >-
      The approved consent form for the principal project site (University of Washington) is published
      with the dataset documentation as AI-READI Consent_Form_Standard_Mod17May2023. The other clinical
      sites operated under IRB reliance and used the same consent form, with minor institution-specific
      language incorporated depending on individual institutional requirements.
    withdrawal_mechanism: >-
      Participants were permitted to withdraw consent at any time and cease study participation. However,
      any data that had been shared or used up to that point would stay in the dataset; this is clearly
      communicated in the consent document.

collection_consents:
  - consent_details: >-
      Potential participants were given the option to read all consent documentation electronically
      (e-consent) before their visit and to give consent with an electronic signature without verbal
      communication with a clinical research coordinator, accessing the documentation through REDCap.
      They could instead request a call back or video conference with a coordinator to ask questions, or
      wait and consent in person at the start of the visit. For most participants, informed consent was
      performed remotely by computer, tablet or smartphone through a link in the hardcopy invitation
      letter or the invitation email. No study-specific questionnaires other than the screening
      questionnaire were made available until consent had been given. Signed electronic consent forms were
      automatically emailed to participants, and additional copies could be requested from the study
      coordinator for the duration of the study.

collection_notifications:
  - notification_details: >-
      Each individual was aware of the data collection: this was not passive data collection or secondary
      use of existing data, but active data collection directly from participants. Individuals identified
      through electronic health record screening were mailed a hardcopy invitation letter and sent an
      invitation email, both personalised, directing them into an online REDCap recruitment interface via
      links, access codes and QR codes, where they could read an overview of the research programme,
      expectations for participation and answers to frequently asked questions, download the informed
      consent document, request a call back from study staff, complete a screening survey and enrol.

consent_revocations:
  - revocation_details: >-
      Participants were permitted to withdraw consent at any time and cease study participation. Any data
      that had been shared or used up to the point of withdrawal remains in the dataset. This limitation is
      clearly communicated in the consent document.

participant_privacy:
  - anonymization_method: >-
      Removal of protected health information according to the HIPAA Privacy Rule Safe Harbor method, plus
      removal of sex, race/ethnicity and medication information from the public release; participant
      directories are named with study ID numbers rather than personal identifiers.
    privacy_techniques: >-
      A robust data dissemination system, a strict licence agreement, and data watermarking for both public
      and controlled sets. Access requires login through a verified ID system. The at-home monitoring
      devices were specifically chosen with participant privacy as a primary concern: they capture no video
      or audio, are not equipped with GPS location monitoring and are not synced with participant-owned
      devices.
    reidentification_risk: >-
      A theoretical risk of future re-identification is acknowledged; AI algorithms can infer individual
      patient characteristics and could facilitate individual-level identification.

participant_compensation:
  - compensation_provided: true
    compensation_amount: 200 USD per study visit
    compensation_rationale: >-
      Study participants received a compensation of 200 USD for the study visit, funded through the NIH
      grant. Payment was provided after receipt of the final data and return of the study devices,
      typically at least two weeks after the visit, and was not prorated. The study also covered reasonable
      costs for parking, public transit or rideshare fees related to the study visit, and transportation
      assistance was offered to participants who reported barriers to transporting themselves.

at_risk_populations:
  special_protections: >-
    Only adults aged 40 years and older who are able to provide their own consent are enrolled, so no
    parental permission, guardian consent or child assent procedures apply. Pregnancy, gestational diabetes
    and type 1 diabetes are exclusionary, and adults older than 85 are excluded. Coordinators alert
    participants to incidental findings before they leave the visit and refer them for emergency or
    ophthalmological care where blood pressure, heart rate or retinal findings (retinal detachment, tumour,
    optic disc oedema) warrant it.

license_and_use_terms:
  data_use_permission: disease_specific_research
  license_terms: >-
    Version 3.0.0 is released under the "AI-READI custom license v2.0" (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17555036),
    a custom licence tailored to enable reuse of the AI-READI dataset for commercial or research purposes
    while imposing strong requirements around data usage, security and secondary sharing to protect study
    participants, especially when data are reused for AI and ML applications. The earlier version of the
    licence (University of Washington Data License Agreement v1.0) grants a non-exclusive, non-transferable
    licence to download, reproduce and use the data and to create derivative works, for research and
    commercial purposes; prohibits transfer or sharing of the data or derivative works with any third party
    other than another licensee bound by identical terms; permits distribution of models, algorithms and
    programs trained on the data provided they do not contain the data and provided reasonable efforts are
    made to minimise the likelihood that data can be memorised, derived, reconstructed or reconstituted;
    prohibits making clinical treatment decisions from the data and any attempt to identify, contact or harm
    data subjects; requires acknowledgement of the source and any funder in publications; requires compliance
    with NIH Genomic Data Sharing Policy security best practices; provides the data "as is" without warranty;
    limits the licensor's aggregate liability to one US dollar; requires indemnification of the licensor; and
    terminates automatically on any breach, after which all copies must be deleted.

ip_restrictions:
  restrictions: >-
    Title to the data and all industrial and intellectual property rights therein remain solely and
    exclusively with the Licensor (University of Washington) and its suppliers; the licensee must not take
    any action inconsistent with such ownership, and any rights not expressly granted are reserved. The
    healthsheet refers users to the licence regarding third-party IP-based restrictions. There are no plans
    by the data-collecting researchers to develop commercial products or file patents, but the dataset is
    made available with a licence permitting commercial use and products or patents may be developed from it
    by others.

regulatory_restrictions:
  confidentiality_level: restricted
  hipaa_compliant: compliant
  regulatory_restrictions: >-
    Licensees must comply with all data security and privacy standards established by the US National
    Institutes of Health under its Genomic Data Sharing (GDS) Policy, and with all applicable laws,
    regulations and restrictions relating to the distribution and use of the data and of models derived from
    it. The healthsheet refers users to the licence regarding export controls and other regulatory
    restrictions.
  other_compliance: >-
    Protected health information was removed according to the HIPAA Privacy Rule Safe Harbor method; the
    DataCite metadata records deIdentHIPAA as true. Access requires login through a verified ID system,
    agreement to use the data only for type 2 diabetes related research, and acceptance of the licence terms.

distribution_formats:
  - name: application/dicom
    description: >-
      DICOM files for retinal photography, OCT, OCTA and FLIO data, following the Digital Imaging and
      Communications in Medicine standard.
    access_urls:
      - https://fairhub.io/datasets/3
  - name: text/csv
    description: >-
      CSV files for clinical data mapped one-to-one to OMOP Common Data Model tables, and for environmental
      sensor and device-derived time series.
    access_urls:
      - https://fairhub.io/datasets/3
  - name: application/json
    description: >-
      JSON files for wearable activity and continuous glucose data following the Open mHealth standard, and
      for root-level metadata files such as dataset_description.json and study_description.json.
    access_urls:
      - https://fairhub.io/datasets/3
  - name: text/markdown
    description: >-
      Markdown files for the README, CHANGELOG and healthsheet documentation included with the dataset.
    access_urls:
      - https://fairhub.io/datasets/3
  - name: WaveForm DataBase (WFDB)
    description: >-
      Files in the WFDB format for 12-lead electrocardiogram signal data and associated annotations.
    access_urls:
      - https://fairhub.io/datasets/3
  - name: Tab-separated values (TSV)
    description: >-
      manifest.tsv files within each datatype directory and the root-level participants.tsv file, following
      the Clinical Dataset Structure specification.
    access_urls:
      - https://fairhub.io/datasets/3

distribution_dates:
  - release_dates:
      - '2024-05-03'
      - '2024-11-08'
      - '2025-11-17'
    description: >-
      Release dates of the three published versions on FAIRhub: version 1.0.0 on 3 May 2024 (pilot data
      collection phase), version 2.0.0 on 8 November 2024 (data collected through the first full year), and
      version 3.0.0 on 17 November 2025 (data collected 19 July 2023 to 1 May 2025). New versions are
      planned approximately once a year.

third_party_sharing:
  - is_shared: true
    description: >-
      The dataset is distributed publicly outside the institutions that created it, through the FAIRhub
      platform at https://fairhub.io. Accessing the dataset requires logging in through a verified ID system,
      agreeing to use the data only for type 2 diabetes related research, and agreeing to the licence terms,
      which set restrictions and obligations for data usage. A more sensitive controlled-access version is
      available only to scientists whose institutions have completed legal and privacy use agreements with
      the AI-READI research programme; requirements for the controlled-access dataset are set by a Data
      Access Committee.

external_resources:
  - external_resources:
      - https://docs.aireadi.org
      - https://aireadi.org
      - https://github.com/AI-READI
      - https://zenodo.org/communities/aireadi
    restrictions: >-
      The dataset is self-contained but relies on the dataset documentation at https://docs.aireadi.org for
      users requiring additional information about its provenance. The documentation is shared under the
      CC-BY 4.0 licence, so there are no restrictions associated with its use.

related_datasets:
  - relationship_type: is_new_version_of
    target_dataset: https://doi.org/10.60775/fairhub.2
  - relationship_type: is_new_version_of
    target_dataset: https://doi.org/10.60775/fairhub.1
  - relationship_type: is_source_of
    target_dataset: https://doi.org/10.60775/fairhub.4

maintainers:
  - role: researcher
    maintainer_details: >-
      The AI-READI team supports and maintains the dataset. The central contact is Aaron Lee
      (contact@aireadi.org); contact information is also provided in the README file included with the
      dataset and via https://aireadi.org/contact.
  - role: third_party
    maintainer_details: >-
      The dataset is hosted on the FAIRhub platform, a cloud-based data management, curation and sharing
      platform running on Microsoft Azure that was developed for this project; the Microsoft AI for Good Lab
      supported the cloud services needed for the project.

updates:
  frequency: Approximately annual
  update_details: >-
    The dataset is released as static versions and is not updated in place. Rather than correcting or
    extending an existing version, new versions are released with additional instances as more study
    participants complete the study visit, approximately once a year. Version 1.0.0 contained 204
    participants (pilot phase), version 2.0.0 contained 1,067 participants (adding 863 from year 2), and
    version 3.0.0 contains 2,280 participants (adding 1,213 from year 3). Changes between versions are
    detailed in the CHANGELOG file included in the dataset and visible at docs.aireadi.org. Enrolment is
    ongoing and future versions with larger numbers of participants are expected.

version_access:
  latest_version_doi: 10.60775/fairhub.3
  versions_available:
    - 1.0.0
    - 2.0.0
    - 3.0.0
  version_details: >-
    All versions are listed on the FAIRhub record: version 1.0.0 (DOI 10.60775/fairhub.1, 3 May 2024),
    version 2.0.0 (DOI 10.60775/fairhub.2, 8 November 2024) and version 3.0.0 (DOI 10.60775/fairhub.3,
    17 November 2025). One version of the dataset documentation is associated with each version of the
    dataset and can be selected from a dropdown at docs.aireadi.org. The FAIRhub page and documentation for
    version 2.0.0 state that that version is no longer accessible and direct users to the latest version.
    A separate smaller "Mini Version" (DOI 10.60775/fairhub.4, 100 participants) is available for pipeline
    development.

retention_limit:
  retention_details: >-
    There are no limits on the retention of the data associated with the instances; participants were not
    told that their data would be retained for a fixed period and then deleted.